hello again england's remaining coveted  restrictions are being lifted from  tomorrow including the legal requirement  to self-isolate the world health  organization special envoy for kovit 19.  that's dr david nabarro who is with us  now um hello to doctors always great to  see you thank you for joining us um has  the government gone too far in lifting  all of these restrictions  i think that actually i'm quite  impressed by  really what's happening because  there is real a stress on continued  prudence in the face of the virus and  that is what's going to be needed to  live with the covid and prevent it from  coming back and causing trouble it's  going to be a bumpy journey moving from  where we were to where we need to be  and i do urge everybody to remember that  this virus is still around it can surge  sometimes dramatically so do what you  can to prevent transmitting it to others  especially if you feel unwell if you're  not required to isolate i still  encourage you to do so i also encourage  you to protect people who are at risk  older people and people with diseases  but perhaps most importantly because  this virus surges do prepare for the  probability that it will surge again but  overall we have to get on with life we  have to live with it and uh  perhaps we we do have to learn to do it  without restrictions from on high we  just got to do it working amongst  ourselves  and what about dropping testing is that  a good idea  well i must say that i've believed in  the british approach to testing being  really right at the front end of what  needs to be done i know people who've  had lateral flow tests and been able to  check their covert status before they go  to work every day and people who use  their covid checks to see whether  they're infected before they go to  social events and it really has been  useful at reducing the risk so i i do  hope that people can still access tests  and that those who really need them and  can't afford them will not be prevented  from doing so  if we don't have testing how do you find  new variants that's very difficult i  think that one of the good pieces of  news that i read from the uk is that  there is a plan to continue the  surveillance systems that have been used  to pick up new variants and uh you can't  actually check for a variant on lateral  flow testing but if you've got a well  organized set set of laboratories that  are doing uh further analysis of test  samples from people around the country  that helps you pick them up so yes  testing i mean if there's not testing  it's very difficult to take the uk  strategy to its limits and i i suppose  i'm hopeful that the people who can't  afford tests will still be able to get  them  but people are not going to go for a pcr  test are they um unless um they think  that they have got covid and the way to  have done that previously was to with  these lateral flow tests so the only way  i'm guessing that we'll pick it up is  through random testing for people who  are traveling  i think it will be uh random testing for  people who are traveling it'll be more  people coming into hospital in a  particular area and picked up with their  covid there it may be gp sounding the  alarm because they see more people being  ill in their surgeries or it may be just  a lot of absenteeism uh about for people  because you do still feel pretty rotten  when you get covered so absenteeism in  schools or in uh workplaces  without testing of the kind that britain  has had it's much harder you know the  virus is the problem people are the  solution if you want to solve the  problem you need to know where the virus  is and so your question about free  testing i know it's government policy  but my my approach would be to try to  keep the tests being available  particularly for those who can't afford  them particularly for those who are at  risk like security staff people working  in public transport and of course health  workers and what would you do about  passenger location forms um doctor i  mean they've been a pain at the best of  times for people and it's been  questionable how effective they have  been and yet despite all of the other  sanctions and regulations being lifted  restrictions they're still in place  yes i i noticed that too  you use passenger locator forms in case  there is a  person with covid picked up on a flight  and you want to find the other  passengers to get hold of them quickly  they're quite a useful part of the  process of tracing where people are when  they're ill  i'd certainly  expect them to continue but i that's why  i also favor maintaining capacity for  contact tracing when you do get a  cluster of cases as a disease outbreak  drops and the numbers of cases reduce  so it becomes more and more important to  be able to do the detective work so that  if you are getting some cases appearing  you can quickly pinpoint where  they're coming from so these kinds of  things like passenger locator forms or  contact tracing systems are particularly  useful in the uh  the downward slope of an outbreak curve  sorry for being technical  perhaps okay but i mean we'd only know  if there was a cluster of cases if  people were poorly enough to either go  to the doctor or to find themselves in  hospital is that not right that's right  so there are parts of what's happening  in not just the uk but in many countries  that are leaving me wondering a bit uh  why they're making these decisions  the basic situation is that as you  emerge from a nasty pandemic or outbreak  the the the emergency is always bumpy  it's always difficult and people are  incredibly frustrated and want to get on  with their lives governments want to get  people moving forward and and the  economic recovery in place but it's  during that time that you really do need  to know what's going on so i suppose if  i had a magic wand  and i wasn't worried about what the  treasury would say i would try to  maintain things like free testing just  through this period until the number of  cases really is down because if you  don't know where the virus is if you  don't know who's ill if you don't know  which parts of the country have got  surges it's super difficult for people  to make informed choices which is what i  understand the uk government and other  european governments are asking their  people to do

